{"date": "2026-10-14", "post_id": 1487715, "title": "Mars and the Beehive Under an Aurora", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-14-mars-and-the-beehive-under-an-aurora/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> What a cosmic coincidence! In <a href=\"https://www.petrhoralek.com/?p=26662\">today\u2019s image</a>, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mars/\">Mars</a> joins the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-april-30-m44-the-beehive-cluster/\">Beehive cluster</a> (<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/explore-the-night-sky/hubble-messier-catalog/messier-44/\">M44</a> or Praesepe) below an <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-march-8-the-aurora-tree/\">auroral curtain</a> and above a horizon of <a href=\"https://youtube.com/shorts/1VXZhqbxsng?si=J9_AydZHXiweXx81\">happy little trees</a> near <a href=\"https://youtu.be/ALGu8Yu_66s?si=HWByw9OqhcsnNX8i\">Lansj\u00e4rv, Sweden</a>. An <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/solar-storms-and-flares/#coronal-mass-ejection\">eruption off the Sun</a> reached Earth on <a href=\"https://www.spaceweather.gov/news/strong-g3-geomagnetic-storms-expected-10-oct-2026\">October 10th, 2026</a> causing strong <a href=\"https://www.spaceweather.gov/phenomena/geomagnetic-storms\">disturbances in Earth\u2019s magnetic field</a>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E3T2ldsdtMg\">Energetic particles</a> from the eruption collide with and energize the molecules in Earth\u2019s atmosphere, which release that extra energy as the colorful <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/auroras/\">aurora</a> captured here. Auroras, or guovssahasat in North S\u00e1mi, is traditionally viewed with respect by the <a href=\"https://sweden.se/life/people/sami-in-sweden\">S\u00e1mi people</a> indigenous to Sweden and other Arctic regions. Coincidentally, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-march-23-ancient-ogunquit-beach-on-mars/\">Mars</a> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apparent_place\">appeared closest</a> to the center of the Beehive cluster in the sky at the time. Although <a href=\"https://texasarchive.org/2024AC06462\">bees have been to space</a>, the Beehive cluster is actually a swarm of young stars. <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2106.13250\">Their rotation periods</a> have helped humanity learn about how <a href=\"http://cfa.harvard.edu/news/stars-spins-reveal-their-ages\">stars spin slower over time</a> as they lose mass through their <a href=\"https://esahubble.org/wordbank/stellar-wind/\">stellar winds</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> open space", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/petrhoralek\">Petr Hor\u00e1lek</a> (<a href=\"https://www.slu.cz/phys/en/\">Institute of Physics in Opava</a>), <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/josef.kujal/\">Josef Kujal</a>, <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/VoBaPhoto/\">Vojan H\u00f6fer</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/petrhoralek\">Petr Hor\u00e1lek</a> (<a href=\"https://www.slu.cz/phys/en/\">Institute of Physics in Opava</a>), <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/josef.kujal/\">Josef Kujal</a>, <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/VoBaPhoto/\">Vojan H\u00f6fer</a>", "alt": "The bright Mars is near the center of the Beehive cluster (M44) in the sky&#039;s horizon. A colorful aurora sits above in the sky, looking like a curtain of light. Dark trees and a river line the foreground.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-14-mars-and-the-beehive-under-an-aurora/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/2026_10_10_PZ_Jesli\u010dky_01_1500px.png?w=1500&h=1000&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 14 - Mars and the Beehive Under an Aurora</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-14-mars-and-the-beehive-under-an-aurora/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: What a cosmic coincidence! In today\u2019s image, Mars joins the Beehive cluster (M44 or Praesepe) below an auroral curtain and above a horizon of happy little trees near Lansj\u00e4rv, Sweden. An eruption off the Sun reached Earth on October 10th, 2026 causing strong disturbances in Earth\u2019s magnetic field. Energetic particles from the eruption collide with and energize the molecules in Earth\u2019s atmosphere, which release that extra energy as the colorful aurora captured here. Auroras, or guovssahasat in North S\u00e1mi, is traditionally viewed with respect by the S\u00e1mi people indigenous to Sweden and other Arctic regions. Coincidentally, Mars appeared closest to the center of the Beehive cluster in the sky at the time. Although bees have been to space, the Beehive cluster is actually a swarm of young stars. Their rotation periods have helped humanity learn about how stars spin slower over time as they lose mass through their stellar winds.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: open space\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"mars and the beehive under an aurora\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Mars and the Beehive Under an Aurora\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-14-mars-and-the-beehive-under-an-aurora/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/2026_10_10_PZ_Jesli\u010dky_01_1500px.png?w=1500&#038;h=1000&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/2026_10_10_PZ_Jesli\u010dky_01_1500px.png?w=1500&#038;h=1000&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"The bright Mars is near the center of the Beehive cluster (M44) in the sky&#039;s horizon. A colorful aurora sits above in the sky, looking like a curtain of light. Dark trees and a river line the foreground.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-14T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-13T22:13:15-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Mars and the Beehive Under an Aurora\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/2026_10_10_PZ_Jesli\u010dky_01_1500px.png?w=1500&#038;h=1000&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 14<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/2026_10_10_PZ_Jesli\u010dky_01_1500px.png\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/2026_10_10_PZ_Jesli\u010dky_01_1500px.png\" alt=\"The bright Mars is near the center of the Beehive cluster (M44) in the sky&#039;s horizon. A colorful aurora sits above in the sky, looking like a curtain of light. Dark trees and a river line the foreground.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Mars and the Beehive Under an Aurora </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/petrhoralek\">Petr Hor\u00e1lek</a> (<a href=\"https://www.slu.cz/phys/en/\">Institute of Physics in Opava</a>), <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/josef.kujal/\">Josef Kujal</a>, <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/VoBaPhoto/\">Vojan H\u00f6fer</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> What a cosmic coincidence! In <a href=\"https://www.petrhoralek.com/?p=26662\">today\u2019s image</a>, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mars/\">Mars</a> joins the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-april-30-m44-the-beehive-cluster/\">Beehive cluster</a> (<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/explore-the-night-sky/hubble-messier-catalog/messier-44/\">M44</a> or Praesepe) below an <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-march-8-the-aurora-tree/\">auroral curtain</a> and above a horizon of <a href=\"https://youtube.com/shorts/1VXZhqbxsng?si=J9_AydZHXiweXx81\">happy little trees</a> near <a href=\"https://youtu.be/ALGu8Yu_66s?si=HWByw9OqhcsnNX8i\">Lansj\u00e4rv, Sweden</a>. An <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/solar-storms-and-flares/#coronal-mass-ejection\">eruption off the Sun</a> reached Earth on <a href=\"https://www.spaceweather.gov/news/strong-g3-geomagnetic-storms-expected-10-oct-2026\">October 10th, 2026</a> causing strong <a href=\"https://www.spaceweather.gov/phenomena/geomagnetic-storms\">disturbances in Earth\u2019s magnetic field</a>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E3T2ldsdtMg\">Energetic particles</a> from the eruption collide with and energize the molecules in Earth\u2019s atmosphere, which release that extra energy as the colorful <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/sun/auroras/\">aurora</a> captured here. Auroras, or guovssahasat in North S\u00e1mi, is traditionally viewed with respect by the <a href=\"https://sweden.se/life/people/sami-in-sweden\">S\u00e1mi people</a> indigenous to Sweden and other Arctic regions. Coincidentally, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-march-23-ancient-ogunquit-beach-on-mars/\">Mars</a> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apparent_place\">appeared closest</a> to the center of the Beehive cluster in the sky at the time. Although <a href=\"https://texasarchive.org/2024AC06462\">bees have been to space</a>, the Beehive cluster is actually a swarm of young stars. <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2106.13250\">Their rotation periods</a> have helped humanity learn about how <a href=\"http://cfa.harvard.edu/news/stars-spins-reveal-their-ages\">stars spin slower over time</a> as they lose mass through their <a href=\"https://esahubble.org/wordbank/stellar-wind/\">stellar winds</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> open space\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-13-desert-stairway-to-heaven/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nKeighley Rockcliffe, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261014/html"}